 * Volume + gap bound (sand-only grids).
 *
 * Take an empty box E that has sand somewhere above it in the same column. A side gate always
 * leaves half a box behind, so some of that sand stays in the column until it has crossed the gate
 * on top of E. It can cross in two ways:
 *   - the agent opens that very gate: a top-down action with s >= 1/2, cost >= 3;
 *   - the door opens by itself because it is pressed from both sides, which needs E to be FULL
 *     first. E can receive at most half a box through its side gate (a side gate only works into an
 *     empty box), so the other half must already have crossed the gate from above.
 * Hence the first crossing is always a paid action on that gate, costing at least 3. The gate is not
 * an outlet and belongs to E alone, so every such box adds 3 on top of the volume bound.
 *
 * Under the alternative "whole pile always follows" rule a half-filled E is enough for the pile to
 * follow, and that half can come from the side gate for 1, so the weight drops to 1.
 * With water or concrete in the grid the argument no longer holds and the gap term is dropped.
 */
function gap(model, cells) {
  let gaps = 0;
  for (let col = 0; col < 2; col++) {
    let sandAbove = false;
